WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
In this lesson, we'll go through making the right choice between built-in and custom tools.

2
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.560
We'll go through the conditions that favor each approach, how a hybrid pattern fits them together,

3
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.840
the security boundaries you need to understand, and the naming and versioning habits that catch

4
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.159
architects that are new to cloud tool ecosystems. Let's get into it.

5
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.920
The decision to build a custom tool starts with a single question. What can't the built-in do?

6
00:00:26.399 --> 00:00:32.080
Capability gap is the name for that distance between what a built-in tool can do and what

7
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:38.000
your workflow actually needs. When the gap is small, reach for the built-in. When it's large,

8
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.759
build custom. The hybrid pattern is what most production systems end up using. Built-ins

9
00:00:43.759 --> 00:00:50.319
handle general purpose work like public research and code transformation. A custom MCP server

10
00:00:50.319 --> 00:00:56.240
handles proprietary data, internal APIs, and regulated records. There's also a naming best

11
00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:02.639
practice worth adopting early. Give your custom tools distinct service-prefixed names so selection

12
00:01:02.639 --> 00:01:08.319
stays unambiguous. Duplicate or invalid tool definitions are generally rejected when you

13
00:01:08.319 --> 00:01:13.919
make the request, so distinct names keep things clean, even when the functionality overlaps a

14
00:01:13.919 --> 00:01:20.639
built-in. Three conditions favor built-in tools, and when all three hold, you should default to

15
00:01:20.639 --> 00:01:27.199
them. First, quick prototyping. Built-ins require no schema work, so you can get a working prototype

16
00:01:27.199 --> 00:01:34.000
in front of stakeholders without a full tool definition. Second, well-fitted scope. If web

17
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.440
search does exactly what your research workflow needs, building a custom search wrapper adds

18
00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:47.599
complexity with no benefit. Third, no internal or regulated data flowing through the tool. Built-ins

19
00:01:47.599 --> 00:01:52.720
run inside Anthropx infrastructure. If your workflow handles data that shouldn't leave your

20
00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:59.199
controlled environment, a built-in isn't the right path. But when the task is public facing, the scope

21
00:01:59.199 --> 00:02:06.160
fits, and speed matters. Built-ins are the faster and more maintainable choice. Four conditions

22
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:12.240
signal that you need a custom tool rather than a built-in. Domain-specific schemas are the first.

23
00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:18.720
Built-ins use Anthropx's fixed schema, so if your workflow needs custom field names, specific types,

24
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:25.520
or domain validation, you define the schema yourself. Internal APIs are the second. Built-ins

25
00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:31.360
are sandboxed away from your private network, so reaching a product catalog service or an internal

26
00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:38.399
database takes a custom MCP server. Regulated data is the third. Healthcare records, financial

27
00:02:38.399 --> 00:02:43.600
transactions, and similar sensitive records should stay in your controlled environment

28
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:49.440
rather than flowing through infrastructure you don't own. Audit trail requirements are the fourth.

29
00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:55.679
Built-ins don't expose per-call logs, so if compliance needs every invocation, its parameters,

30
00:02:55.679 --> 00:03:02.000
and its response recorded, custom tools give you that control. The hybrid pattern is the

31
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:07.839
architecture most mature cloud deployments converge on. The idea is to let built-in tools

32
00:03:07.839 --> 00:03:14.479
handle the general purpose work and let custom MCP servers handle the proprietary or regulated data

33
00:03:14.479 --> 00:03:21.119
layer. Web search handles public research. Code execution handles data transformation on inputs

34
00:03:21.119 --> 00:03:28.000
Cloud already has. A custom MCP server handles the internal database queries, the regulated record

35
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:34.080
lookups, and the domain-specific API calls. The two layers don't interfere with each other as long

36
00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.679
as you use distinct tool names. The main design consideration is routing. You need your system

37
00:03:39.679 --> 00:03:46.240
prompt to be clear about which tool Cloud should reach for in each situation. Ambiguous instructions

38
00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:50.399
lead to the model guessing, and guessing means inconsistent behavior in production.

39
00:03:51.679 --> 00:03:56.720
The security boundary depends on whether a tool is a server tool or a client tool,

40
00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:02.320
and this is where people get it backwards. Server tools like web search and code execution

41
00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:08.639
run on Anthropx infrastructure. That environment has no path to your private network and no access

42
00:04:08.639 --> 00:04:15.440
to secrets stored in your systems. Their blast radius is bounded by design. Client tools are

43
00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:22.079
the opposite story. Bash, text editor, and computer run in your own environment because

44
00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:27.600
your application executes them. They can and do reach whatever private resources you expose to

45
00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.839
them. So the rule isn't that built-ins are always sandboxed. Match each tool to the trust boundary

46
00:04:33.839 --> 00:04:40.559
you intend. Server tools stay outside your walls. Client tools run inside them, which is why you

47
00:04:40.559 --> 00:04:47.600
scope what they can touch and never hand them more access than the task needs. Naming is a small

48
00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:53.119
decision that pays off across a whole tool set, so it's worth being deliberate about. The best

49
00:04:53.119 --> 00:04:59.600
practice is to give every custom tool a distinct service-prefixed name. Something like crm

50
00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:05.119
underscore lookup or billing underscore refund tells you in the model which service owns the

51
00:05:05.119 --> 00:05:12.160
tool at a glance. This keeps selection unambiguous, even when a custom tool's functionality overlaps a

52
00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:17.440
built-in. You don't have to worry about a silent collision routing to the wrong implementation

53
00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:23.200
because duplicate or invalid tool definitions are generally rejected when you make the request,

54
00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:28.959
not something that fails quietly at runtime. So the guidance is straightforward. Name for clarity,

55
00:05:28.959 --> 00:05:34.959
prefix by service, and let distinct names do the disambiguation work up front. It's one of the

56
00:05:34.959 --> 00:05:41.279
cheapest ways to keep a growing tool set predictable. Built-in tools evolve. Anthropic

57
00:05:41.279 --> 00:05:47.760
can change query construction logic, update result formats, adjust rate limits, or modify the behavior

58
00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.959
of any built-in between model releases. You shouldn't assume that because a tool name is

59
00:05:52.959 --> 00:05:58.399
constant, the behavior is constant. The way to get stable behavior is to pin it through your system

60
00:05:58.399 --> 00:06:04.320
prompt instructions. Instead of relying on web search to always format citations a certain way,

61
00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:10.640
instruct the model explicitly on what format you expect and how to handle deviations. Test

62
00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:16.399
built-in tool output in a staging environment after each model update. Treat built-in tools

63
00:06:16.399 --> 00:06:22.720
the way you'd treat any third-party dependency, version aware, tested on updates, and not assumed

64
00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:28.079
to be stable without verification. That mindset prevents subtle regressions from appearing in

65
00:06:28.079 --> 00:06:34.959
production after a model rollout. Treat this as a question about your data before treating it like a

66
00:06:34.959 --> 00:06:41.440
question about tooling. Can the built-in tool reach far enough and can this material or data safely

67
00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:48.399
leave your own ecosystem? If you can answer both clearly, the call tends to make itself. It's rarely

68
00:06:48.399 --> 00:06:54.799
an all-or-nothing decision since general research can ride on built-ins while your regulated records

69
00:06:54.799 --> 00:07:00.320
and data stay behind on your server. That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

